e the lane's own reservation. It reads no retirement record at all. Called directly with a reservation of SR-006 for WI-401, an added SR-006 passes. That holds even after the lane retired SR-006, so the landing would hold both a retirement record and a live SR-006. The only retired-number test uses SR-003, outside the range, so the range check refuses it anyway. Nothing else in the retirement checks catches a live id that also has a retirement record.\n- **Plausible under §6.** Every step is a supported route: reserve, author, retire with `retire.py`, author again. The re-use is also exactly what an agent would do. This lane's own prompt tells the spine author to draft from the reserved range, and its PROCESS.md text leans toward minting a new id when a row is replaced. With a one-id range, the replacement reuses the freed number.\n- **Why FIX and not SUCCESSOR:**\n  - **The approved SR:** it breaks SR-174's \"an identity freed by a deletion is never re-issued\".\n  - **The spec:** it breaks the spec's own landing-table row, \"a retired number re-used\".\n  - **The completion criteria:** it is a gap against \"every row of the landing table is driven\", which rules out a successor.\n- **The fix:** on both callers, refuse an added id that the landing or its parent records as retired, inside the range as well as outside it. Keep the existing refusals. Add a real reserve, retire, re-author regression that fails on today's code. Amend LLR-363, TC-402 and IF-101 through their own authority; SR-174 already states the rule.\n\nThe commit hooks passed, but again reported that the `format` check didn't run (ruff isn't installed). I committed only the verdict, so `008-ADJUDICATE-7f27643.md.requested` is still untracked.","ttft_ms":11570,"type":"result","duration_ms":223206,"uuid":"cf89e3ca-ea0c-49ac-9040-fb8a64223834","ttft_stream_ms":10371,"time_to_request_ms":139,"first_content_frame_ms":10371,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}]
